WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.860
Namor the Sub-Mariner is a pretty cool comic
book character.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:11.860
The first real anti-hero in comics, he's fought alongside Captain America, has a wealth

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:16.090
of exotic powers, and rocks a form-fitting
speedo.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.220
Diehard fans have clamored for a Namor movie
for years.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.740
And with complex rights issues surrounding
the character reportedly resolved, it would

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.710
seem like the time is finally right.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.349
But so far there's been no sign of Marvel's
oldest superhero.

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:36.960
Here's a look at why Marvel won't give Namor
a movie.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.440
Too many worlds

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.460
The Marvel Cinematic Universe has explored
other dimensions, distant worlds, and even

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.330
the realms of the gods.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:50.000
Yet in all of those travels, and through all
the world-threatening catastrophes we've seen

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.820
on screen, the only sign of Namor's underwater
kingdom of Atlantis was a brief Easter egg

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.120
on a map.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.370
And that might be for good reason.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.240
Once Marvel introduces a race of superhuman
merfolk led by a benevolent monarch, fans

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.250
are going to start wondering just why they
aren't, you know... doing anything.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:15.140
Think about your buddy who always asks "why
don't the Avengers just show up" every time

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.120
you see a solo Marvel movie.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.320
With Namor in the picture, everyone's going
to ask why his army of fish people don't swim

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.200
in and save the day.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.800
And seriously: it'll stop being funny after
the second time.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.800
Impossible backstory

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:36.270
Namor has a really cool origin — and it
pretty much doesn't make any sense in the

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.340
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.850
The Prince of Atlantis has supposedly been
around since World War II, fighting alongside

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.840
Cap and the Invaders.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.770
But we know from Captain America: The First
Avenger that no fish folk were anywhere to

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:51.770
be seen.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:58.450
And explaining why Namor hasn't even shown
up at any point in the last 75 years of MCU

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:03.619
continuity would be even trickier than Marvel's
efforts to thread the Winter Soldier into

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:05.260
historical events.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.819
With SHIELD and Hydra both aggressively tracking
superhuman threats, you'd think someone would

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:14.799
have noticed Namor and Atlantis at some point.

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:19.469
Explaining why they haven't appeared on anyone's
radar to this point makes Namor's story that

00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:21.579
much more difficult to tell.

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:23.829
Worst Kryptonite ever

00:02:23.829 --> 00:02:28.719
In many ways, our superheroes are measured
by their weaknesses, like the all-powerful

00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:31.689
Superman being taken out by Kryptonite.

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:40.269
Or imps from the fifth-dimension!

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:45.889
But while Kryptonite and wacky imps are in
relatively short supply, Namor has a much

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:48.980
more common weakness: air.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:50.860
Yes, that's right.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:56.810
You see, Namor gets his powers from water,
so the longer he's out of water, the weaker

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.590
and weaker he gets.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.889
"The sea!

00:02:59.889 --> 00:03:02.450
I must reach the sea!"

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:08.660
If that's not enough, he's also weakened when
he comes in contact with polluted water, which

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.549
means he's pretty much doomed in today's world.

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:16.660
When your hero needs to wear a Camelbak in
order to keep from dying, chances are he's

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.870
going to have a really short career.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.879
Similarity to Aquaman

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:26.749
Speaking of aquatic superheroes who gain their
powers from water, one of the primary reasons

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:32.349
we'll never see a Namor movie is that DC is
beating Marvel to the punch by releasing a

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:34.389
movie about Aquaman first.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.260
"Arthur Curry.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.220
I hear you can talk to fish?"

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:45.220
They're essentially the same character — superstrong
kings of Atlantis who can communicate with

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.760
marine life — the only differences being
that Aquaman is a nerd and Namor is a stud.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.510
It's ironic that, even though Aquaman is much
better known, he's actually a total ripoff

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.099
of Namor, who first appeared in comics pages
two years earlier.

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:04.949
Still, that's not going to matter much to
film audiences.

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:12.389
To them, Namor's going to seem like the copycatfish,
and that's a big blow to his film future.

00:04:12.389 --> 00:04:13.389
The mutant problem

00:04:13.389 --> 00:04:19.140
While Marvel Chief Creative Officer Joe Quesada
stated in the summer of 2016 that Marvel has

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:24.100
regained the film rights to Namor, it's still
complicated by other rights issues.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:29.409
You might have heard that stories set in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe can't mention mutants

00:04:29.409 --> 00:04:33.690
— because the rights to mutants live at
20th Century Fox with the X-Men.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:39.430
That's why the Avengers version of Quicksilver
was a "miracle" created by HYDRA.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:46.039
And this is a problem for Namor, because Marvel
Comics has long marketed him as "the world's

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:51.139
first mutant," explaining his super-strength
and flight as genetic mutations.

00:04:51.139 --> 00:04:56.169
Combined with the other issues surrounding
his backstory, it seems like any version of

00:04:56.169 --> 00:05:02.560
Namor we might see on screen would essentially
have to be an entirely different character.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:03.979
The terrorism thing

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:06.650
Remember when we called Namor an anti-hero?

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:13.090
Well, he also sometimes just veers straight
into the realm of flat-out villainy.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:18.770
In many of his earliest stories, he vented
his rage on arrogant surface dwellers by randomly

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.650
trashing their cities.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:26.900
Later, when he thought Atlantis had been destroyed
by nuclear testing, he vowed to destroy all

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:34.680
of humanity, attempting to level New York
using a giant walking whale monster even after

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.960
he learned his people were actually totally
fine.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.560
One time he even destroyed Black Panther's
home country of Wakanda, just for funsies.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.909
Namor's moral ambiguity is hardwired into
the character's appeal, but superhero cinema

00:05:49.909 --> 00:05:54.720
and Marvel movies in particular don't leave
much room for this kind of nuance.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.340
Without it, though, Namor is just another
guy, albeit a guy in a scaly thong.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:05.630
Which is fine for Magic Mike 3, but maybe
not so great for Avengers 3.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.180
He makes for a better foil

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:12.960
Historically, Namor has been a great character
for others to play off of, but not all that

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.490
interesting on his own.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:19.520
He's a cool rival for the affections of the
Invisible Woman, an intriguing ally for the

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.590
X-Men, a complex villain for the Avengers,
and a great foil for the original Human Torch.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:31.270
But his solo adventures have been kind of
lackluster, with his last comic book series

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.889
running just 11 issues before it was cancelled.

00:06:34.889 --> 00:06:40.310
It's possible Marvel could find a way to use
Namor as a member of a team like the Avengers,

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.570
or as a villain for one of their TV shows
or something.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.900
But as the lead in a solo feature film?

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.760
If history is our guide, that seems unlikely.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:52.500
The MCU that could have been

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:57.639
Regardless of the impossibility of his own
movie, it's fun to imagine what Namor might

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:00.289
have brought to the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:05.759
It would be cool to see Namor shouting "Imperius
Rex!" while repelling the next round of alien

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:08.569
invaders, or punching Thanos in his stupid
purple face.

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:14.949
Alas, the Namor movie is less likely to happen
than a gritty cinematic reboot of Howard the

00:07:14.949 --> 00:07:15.949
Duck.

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:20.490
In the finest Marvel Comics tradition, his
fans are simply left to imagine "what if?"

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:21.490
Thanks for watching!

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:24.379
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:24.379 --> 00:07:27.220
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

